---
// Telemetry's story, from Ephraim Atta-Duncan's answers (2026-10-08); the
// page counts and history are from the public telemetry-dev/docs repository
// and telemetry.dev/docs's sitemap.
import { NEW_TAB_ATTRS } from "blume/core/new-tab.ts";
import Quote from "../_customers/Quote.astro";
import StoryPage from "../_customers/StoryPage.astro";
import { storyById } from "../_customers/customers.ts";
---

<StoryPage story={storyById("telemetry")}>
  <p>
    <a href="https://telemetry.dev" {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>Telemetry</a> helps
    developers find failures, slow calls, and unexpected costs in AI
    applications and agents. An AI request can pass through several model
    calls, tools, and retrievals before it produces an answer. Telemetry brings
    those steps into one trace, with latency, errors, token usage, and cost
    estimates, so developers can see where a request slowed down or failed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Telemetry is open source and built on OpenTelemetry. It works with its own
    TypeScript and Python SDKs or an existing OpenTelemetry setup, and its docs
    cover all of it: a quickstart, both SDKs, the ingest API, and a guide for
    each integration, from the Vercel AI SDK and LangChain to Claude Code and
    Cursor.
  </p>

  <h2>Starting from scratch</h2>
  <p>
    Telemetry had no docs to move. Getting them started meant making a few
    choices early: where they would run, how much work hosting them would take,
    and how well coding agents could work with them. Blume fit all three, and
    became Telemetry's first docs platform.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ephraim Atta-Duncan, Telemetry's co-founder, committed the first version on
    August 7, 2026: 29 pages covering the SDKs, the platform, the ingest API,
    and the first 11 integrations.
  </p>

  <h2>Hosted where Telemetry chose</h2>
  <p>
    The first thing that stood out was control. Blume is open source and free
    to use, and it builds docs into static files that any host can serve. Being
    free mattered, but being easy to self-host is what made that freedom
    practical.
  </p>

  <Quote
    avatar="/customers/ephraim-atta-duncan.webp"
    name="Ephraim Atta-Duncan"
    role="Co-founder, Telemetry"
  >
    Blume gave us the freedom we wanted for our docs: an open-source framework
    we could run ourselves, wherever we chose to host it.
  </Quote>

  <p>
    Telemetry serves its docs from Cloudflare as static assets, with no server
    to run. They started at docs.telemetry.dev, and a month in, the team moved
    them next to the product at telemetry.dev/docs. That took two settings: a{" "}
    <a href="/docs/deployment#subpath-deploys"><code>base</code></a> of{" "}
    <code>/docs</code> in <code>blume.config.ts</code>, which moves the whole
    site under the path, from every page to its sitemap, llms.txt, and Open
    Graph images, and a route for the path in Cloudflare. Old links to the
    subdomain redirect to the new address.
  </p>

  <h2>Built to work with agents</h2>
  <p>
    Telemetry helps developers understand what happens inside AI applications
    and agents, so a docs framework that works well with agents made sense for
    the product. Blume's CLI mattered as much as where the docs could run.
  </p>

  <Quote
    avatar="/customers/ephraim-atta-duncan.webp"
    name="Ephraim Atta-Duncan"
    role="Co-founder, Telemetry"
  >
    We wanted coding agents to have clear information to work with. Blume's CLI
    and agent integrations were a big part of why we chose it.
  </Quote>

  <p>
    Blume's CLI reports problems where an agent can act on them:{" "}
    <code>blume build</code> fails on a page that doesn't parse instead of
    shipping without it, and{" "}
    <a href="/docs/cli/doctor"><code>blume doctor</code></a> and{" "}
    <a href="/docs/cli/validate"><code>blume validate</code></a> print JSON for
    an agent to read. Blume's <a href="/docs/advanced/skills">agent skills</a>{" "}
    teach Claude Code, Codex, and Cursor how a Blume site fits together.
  </p>

  <h2>An integration hub</h2>
  <p>
    The docs grew with the product. Telemetry has added 9 integration guides
    since launch, for 20 across frameworks, model providers, and coding agents,
    and the docs now start from them: send a first trace, then pick the
    integration that matches your stack.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two{" "}
    <a href="/docs/configuration/customization#component-overrides"
      >component overrides</a
    > carry each integration's logo through the docs: one puts it beside the page
    title, and an integration card built on Blume's own Card shows it on the
    overview grid. The rest of the look comes from Blume's theme options, a
    black-and-white accent and a medium corner radius, plus 36 lines of CSS for
    the logos.
  </p>
  <p>
    Agents get the same hub without the markup. Every page has a{" "}
    <a href="/docs/discoverability/markdown">Markdown version</a>, and the site
    publishes <a href="/docs/discoverability/llms-txt">llms.txt</a> and
    llms-full.txt. A{" "}
    <a href="/docs/discoverability/markdown#custom-component-serializers"
      >serializer</a
    > in <code>blume.config.ts</code> turns each integration card into a plain Markdown
    link with its one-line description, so an agent reading the overview gets a
    clean list of all 20 guides.
  </p>

  <h2>The results</h2>
  <p>
    Telemetry's docs have been on Blume since the first page. They run as static
    files on Cloudflare at telemetry.dev/docs, next to the product, and every
    page has a Markdown copy for agents.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">What</th>
        <th scope="col">Number</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Pages</th><td>38, up from 29 at launch</td></tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Integration guides</th><td>20, up from 11 at launch</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Component overrides</th><td>2, for integration logos</td>
      </tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Custom CSS</th><td>36 lines</td></tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Servers to run</th><td>0, static files on Cloudflare</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The docs are live at{" "}
    <a href="https://telemetry.dev/docs" {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>telemetry.dev/docs</a
    >, and their source is{" "}
    <a href="https://github.com/telemetry-dev/docs" {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}
      >public on GitHub</a
    >. To start docs of your own, follow the{" "}
    <a href="/docs/quickstart">quickstart</a>.
  </p>
</StoryPage>
